/* Architecture Decision Record: ADR-004 — Logical Architecture Coupling Strategy
 *
 * Documents the coupling strategy (SYSMOD Tool 11.7) applied when deriving the
 * Logical Architecture (SM-PRD-20) from the Functional Architecture (SM-PRD-18)
 * and the Base Architecture (SM-PRD-07). Required before Logical Architecture
 * modeling begins, per KnowledgeSource/integration/crossmapping/
 * logical-architecture.md §5 step 2.
 *
 * arc42 section: ARC-09 Architecture Decisions.
 */
package ADR_004_LogicalCoupling {
    private import BaseArchitecture::*;
    private import FunctionalArchitecture::*;
    // Top-20 fix #17 (finalReviewReport.md corrective pass): 'ArchDecisionMeta'
    // is now declared once in ADR_Common and imported here instead of being
    // re-declared locally -- see that file's header for rationale.
    private import ADR_Common::*;

    package ADR_004 {
        @ArchDecisionMeta {
            decisionId   = "ADR-004";
            title        = "Loose coupling (allocation) between Functional and Logical Architecture; strong coupling (specialization) between Logical and Base Architecture where a 1:1 counterpart exists";
            status       = "accepted";
            context      = "SYSMOD Tool 11.7 requires an explicit coupling decision before Logical Architecture (SM-PRD-20) modeling begins. Per the Logical Architecture cross-mapping (KnowledgeSource/integration/crossmapping/logical-architecture.md §1 Dependencies), 'the Functional Architecture and the Logical Architecture are always loosely coupled by an allocate relationship' -- this is a fixed rule, not a project choice. The coupling between Logical Architecture and Base Architecture (SM-PRD-07), however, IS a project choice: BaseArchitecture::ShutterController already defines five candidate parts (ControlUnit, MotorDriver, WallSwitchInterface, RemoteInterface, PowerModule) that were established at project start (SM-PRD-07) independently of the functional decomposition (SM-PRD-18, nine Functional Elements).";
            decision     = "(1) FUNCTIONAL-TO-LOGICAL: loose coupling via 'allocation def FunctionToLogicalComponent { allocate <FE> to <LogicalComponent>; }' in LogicalArchitecture/FunctionalAllocation.sysml -- no Logical component part def specializes a Functional Element part def. (2) LOGICAL-TO-BASE: strong coupling (':>') is used for the four Logical components with an unambiguous 1:1 Base Architecture counterpart: LogicalControlUnit :> BaseArchitecture::ControlUnit; LogicalMotorDrive :> BaseArchitecture::MotorDriver; LogicalLocalRequestInterface :> BaseArchitecture::WallSwitchInterface; LogicalRemoteRequestInterface :> BaseArchitecture::RemoteInterface. (3) BaseArchitecture::PowerModule has NO Logical Architecture counterpart: no Functional Element performs power conversion/distribution behavior (it is a physical/product-layer concern only), so it is intentionally excluded from the Logical Architecture and reappears only from Product Architecture (SM-PRD-21) onward. (4) Two Logical components have NO Base Architecture counterpart and are therefore modeled as plain 'part def' (no ':>'): LogicalMovementSupervisor (movementSupervisionFunctions, timeCountingFunctions -- a timer-based supervisory capability introduced by ADR-003, orthogonal to command processing) and LogicalUserNotification (userNotificationFunctions -- explicitly called out in FunctionalArchitecture.sysml as 'decoupled from control logic to allow independent Logical Architecture realization, e.g., LED, buzzer, or display indicator'). LogicalDiagnostics (diagnosticsFunctions) is likewise a plain 'part def' pending a dedicated UC_04 elaboration pass. (5) The single requestAcquisitionFunctions Functional Element is allocated to TWO Logical components (LogicalLocalRequestInterface, LogicalRemoteRequestInterface) because Base Architecture already separates local and remote reception into independently replaceable parts; the allocation def documents this 1-to-2 split explicitly with rationale rather than merging the two Base parts into one Logical component.";
            consequences = "1. Any change to BaseArchitecture::ControlUnit/MotorDriver/WallSwitchInterface/RemoteInterface propagates automatically to the corresponding Logical component (strong coupling risk/benefit). 2. Changes to Functional Architecture (e.g., adding a new FE) do NOT automatically update the Logical Architecture -- the allocation def must be manually revisited (loose-coupling trade-off, accepted per the fixed FA-LA rule). 3. PowerModule remains dormant until Product Architecture; any future FE requiring explicit power-sequencing behavior would require revisiting this decision. 4. LogicalMovementSupervisor and LogicalUserNotification, having no Base counterpart, must still be included as 'part' usages in the top-level Logical composite and validated against System Context boundary ports, since they are not automatically covered by BaseArchitecture::ShutterController's five parts. 5. RE-VALIDATION FLAG (formal Logical Architecture review, SysMod Tool 11.6 Zigzag Pattern finding): the four strongly-coupled Logical components (LogicalControlUnit, LogicalMotorDrive, LogicalLocalRequestInterface, LogicalRemoteRequestInterface) have their shape predetermined by Base Architecture's project-start 5-part split rather than emerging purely from Functional Architecture cohesion analysis -- most visibly, LogicalControlUnit's 4-FE concentration mirrors BaseArchitecture::ControlUnit's already-broad scope. This is accepted as-is per this ADR's rationale, but is NOT a permanently-settled decision: this coupling strategy MUST be explicitly re-checked (not silently carried forward) whenever BaseArchitecture or FunctionalArchitecture changes materially, since a strong-coupling change to Base propagates automatically and unreviewed into the Logical layer while a Functional Architecture change does not (asymmetric propagation risk, consequence #1 vs #2 above).";
            alternatives = "A. Strong-couple every Logical component to a Base Architecture part, forcing an artificial 1:1 split of ControlUnit into control-logic/config/memory sub-parts at the Base layer -- rejected because Base Architecture is a stable baseline (SystemArchitecture_Taxonomy.sysml line 9-10: 'must not change once concrete architecture packages depend on it'). B. Loose-couple Logical to Base as well (allocation def only) -- rejected for the four clear 1:1 cases because strong coupling better preserves the intentional design decisions already captured in Base Architecture (e.g., ControlUnit 'processes commands and determines shutter movement behaviour' is a direct logical realization target, not merely an allocation target).";
            rationale    = "This decision follows Tool 11.7 exactly for the FA-LA relationship (fixed loose coupling) while making a deliberate, documented per-component choice for the LA-BA relationship, maximizing reuse of the already-stable Base Architecture where it cleanly matches and avoiding forced/artificial specialization where it does not (movement supervision, notification, diagnostics -- all identified during Functional Architecture analysis as cohesive capabilities without a Base Architecture precedent).";
        }
    }
} // package ADR_004_LogicalCoupling
